  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 224 AM EST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nEarly this morning large scale troughing remained over eastern \nCanada extending southwest into the Great Lakes with multiple \nembedded shortwaves moving south from Ontario. Thus far tonight the \nshortwaves were still failing to produce enough moisture and lift in \nthe DGZ to consistently support dendrites, with a lot of snow \npellets observed along with low snow ratios, around 5:1 at the \nWFO Marquette office in Negaunee Township. Surface observations \nshowed temperatures in the upper 20s to low 30s inland with mid \nto upper 30s along the immediate Lake Superior shoreline. Radar \nand satellite continued to show LES bands in the north wind \nsnowbelts though increased low level stability was starting to \nlimit their extent inland, diminishing before reaching the \nsouthern counties. A shortwave could clearly be seen in the \nwater vapor imagery rotating south through Ontario into the \neastern part of Lake Superior which will impact the weather for \nthe next several hours. \n\nLES is expected to continue in the north wind snowbelts through the \nmorning hours today. As the aforementioned shortwave moves through \nthe U.P. there could be an uptick in lake effect snow showers over \nthe next 2 to 6 hours; satellite imagery indicates deeper upstream \nmoisture and forecast soundings support this. This will result in a \nperiod of deeper moisture and lift into the DGZ. That being said \nstill think snow ratios will continue to run on the lower side \noverall (5:1 to 9:1), especially with the marginally cold low levels \nand sfc. limiting snowfall amounts. Additional snowfall through the \nmorning is expected to be in the 1-3\" range over the north wind \nsnowbelts, highest over the higher terrain of Marquette and Baraga \ncounties. Lower amounts, less than an inch, can be expected near the \nLake Superior shoreline and eastern U.P. do to temperatures staying \nabove freezing. The wet, dense nature of the snow will impact travel \nand those out driving this morning should leave themselves extra \ntime to reach their destination. Temperatures should warm above \nfreezing for most locations today so expect impacts for any \nremaining snow showers to be minimal this afternoon. Inversion \nheights start to collapse late in the afternoon as well, which could \ntransition any lingering showers to rain with the loss of \ntemperatures cold enough to support ice nucleation (warmer than -\n8C). A few light lake effect showers will linger over the eastern \nU.P. on Saturday. \n\nMuch quieter weather is in store from Sunday through the first half \nof next week as high pressure builds in over the Great Lakes and \nmuch of the eastern CONUS. This looks to keep Upper Michigan dry \nthrough at least Wednesday, accompanied by temperatures climbing \nback into the 40s to 50s from Monday onward, well above seasonal \nnorms for mid November. Guidance does hint at another system \nbringing precipitation back to the UP towards the latter half of \nnext week, but confidence in specific details is low at this time.",
